<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baji Rao I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1700">1700</a>–<a href="/years/1740">1740</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baji Rao I was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwa</a>, or chief minister, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> state from 1720 to 1740. A brilliant cavalry commander, he led rapid campaigns that extended Maratha power deep into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, raiding as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. His expansion made the Peshwa the dominant power within the Maratha Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baji-rao-i.jpg" alt="Baji Rao I" width="288" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bajirao_I_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Palkhed in 1728 the <b>Peshwa Baji Rao I</b> outmanoeuvred Nizam-ul-Mulk and forced him to recognise Maratha rights to collect revenue (chauth and sardeshmukhi) in the Deccan, and at the turn of 1737–1738 Baji Rao defeated him again near Bhopal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Peshwa Balaji Vishwanath (1713–1720) and his son <b>Baji Rao I</b> (1720–1740) drove expansion north into Malwa, Gujarat and Bundelkhand, defeating the Nizam at Palkhed (1728) and Bhopal (1737) and raiding the outskirts of Delhi in 1737.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 1730s the Marathas under the <b>Peshwa Baji Rao I</b> and later the Scindia and Holkar houses exacted heavy tribute, nobles feuded among themselves and the treasury was exhausted; in 1810 the young princess Krishna Kumari was poisoned to avert a war between rival suitors from Jaipur…</blockquote><a href="/empire/udaipur-state">Udaipur State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Dewas Junior shares its origin with Dewas Senior: the brothers Tukoji Rao and Jivaji Rao Puar received Dewas around 1728 after campaigning in Malwa with Peshwa <b>Baji Rao I</b>. The Junior Branch descends from Jivaji Rao, and the two lines became separate states holding interlocking villages.</blockquote><a href="/state/dewas-junior">Dewas Junior</a></li><li><blockquote>The brothers Tukoji Rao and Jivaji Rao Puar came to Malwa with the Peshwa <b>Baji Rao I</b> and received Dewas around 1728.</blockquote><a href="/state/dewas-senior">Dewas Senior</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1728–32 the Peshwa <b>Baji Rao I</b> confirmed Anand Rao in Dhar, a city with a long royal past as the 11th-century capital of the Paramara king Bhoja.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhar-state">Dhar State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sinnar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.85,74&amp;year=1726" aria-label="Sinnar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6547 1429 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Baji Rao I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6997 1699h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6997 1699h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.85,74&amp;year=1726">What was here in 1726? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
